The path ahead is occupied. Something wild is sitting in the middle of it — deliberately, the way furniture sits. It saw you coming. It has decided about you.
It won't fight. It just sits down and starts telling you about its week.
[It plants itself, glowing faintly, patient. It came into this world to close wounds, not open them. You can field it or you can leave it home; you cannot make it a blade.]
It refuses to raise a claw. There's a dignity in it that you find faintly accusatory.
Strange thing about the ones that won't fight: the wild predators bow to them anyway. A pacifist that survives long enough in this world is making a point the rest of the bestiary can't argue with — and somewhere it knows it.
[When it finally stands, it does not leave. It falls in beside you, the way a decision does.]
